**Read:** [Manufacture of Organic Fine Chemicals, BREF OFC](../sources/Organic-Fine-Chemicals-BREF.lingua#r_0DH473049K4WX7HAAY1P7SDS3K), the complete solvent/catalyst/reagent recovery and repeated-operation methods; [Production of Chlor-alkali, BREF CAK](../sources/Chlor-Alkali-Production-BREF.lingua#r_69QNQ29VT4NA2MXHTVWDNP8T2W), the entire relevant brine/reagent return and product-system account; [The E factor 25 years on — Roger A. Sheldon](../sources/E-Factor-Sheldon.lingua#r_37VYJ12P4GGC89VRW44RMBK0AT), the full solvent, catalysis and material-intensity comparison.

Recovering a solvent, reagent, catalyst or useful product returns a material with a new composition and history. Recovery fraction, product quality, regeneration demand and repeated-cycle behavior are separate quantities. A circulating stream is not perfectly equivalent to fresh feed by definition.

Trace contaminants and degradation products through each cycle. A selective loss or reaction can alter composition over time; a purge or reprocessing step may be necessary to keep the useful state. Increasing nominal recovery can increase energy, impurity carryover or maintenance and change the downstream product account.

Compare a recovery arrangement with a defined alternative on the same accepted chemical outputs and time basis. Internal circulation must not be counted repeatedly as new feed or product. Include return logistics, storage, characterization, cleanup, remaining wastes and the uncertainty of the measured recovery.

**Work:** reconstruct a full public solvent, catalyst or reagent recovery loop. Independently solve its constituent and inventory accounts, test long-term accumulation and compare a documented regeneration/replacement policy. Explain which primary chemical/physical and analytical observations establish that the recovered material remains suitable, and which result would challenge the claimed system advantage.

**Continue:** [Compare chemical routes with compatible metrics and explicit useful outcomes](Atom-economy-yield-mass-intensity-and-life-cycle-boundaries.lingua#r_4FRG83GXJGE91RRZP86NXEEG4K).

